azgor wrote:
> [1]: Characters with very low dexterity should have intrinsic fumbling.
> --Grunt

Makes sense, and, since all characters start with at least 7 dexterity,
you'd have to work to run into this.  And, of course, if this were
implemented, gauntlets of fumbling could act similar to dunce
caps--they don't cause you to fumble directly, but they set your
dexterity to 3, which amounts to almost the same thing (would this
affect spell casting?)

> [2]: Vorpal Blade against jabberwocks should produce the yafm: "Thou
> hast slain the Jabberwock!" as opposed to the normal death message.
> --Grunt

As has been pointed out, this is almost but not quite the correct
quote.  That said, I still think it's a good idea. :-)

> [4]: If a soldier or any variation thereof (lieutenant, sergeant,
> captain) turns to flee, any neighboring soldiers should attack him/her
> while shouting "Deserter!". --aristotle73

...unless the neighboring soldier is also fleeing.  And, of course,
soliders of the same rank should have a lower chance of doing this, and
soldiers should never turn on their superiors.

> [5]: re: ideas[4]; soldiers should not flee simply because their HP
> gets low. --workimer

...if there's a higher-ranking soldier around.  If no one's ordering
you to the battle, why not run away?  :-)

> [6]: Pets should be much less likely than other monsters to "back away
> skeptically" when you try to #untrap them, and peaceful monsters less
> likely than hostile ones. --PsyMar

As has been stated, this makes so much sense that it's a surprise it's
not already in the game.

> [9]: Monsters that cast spells should have, say 50% change of carrying
> the spellbooks for the spells they know. --ijon

Ooh, finally a chance to get a spellbook of destroy armor!  And
malignant aura!  And summon insects/snakes!  Whoo!

Not.

> [10]: Monsters should have a WIS stat for judging whether to attack you
> or run away from you (or if they're your pet, other monsters) --PsyMar

Pets already have this sort of thing, though it's a binary
consideration.  I'm not sure I'd like it for monsters--imagine Yeenoghu
stealing the amulet and deciding that your extinctionist tourist is too
powerful, and constantly teleporting to the far side of the level...

> [11]: A wand of "order" or "rearranging" or somesuch. Zap it in a
> direction and the monsters within x squares will rearrange, altering
> the order in which they're lined up --offipso

And zapping it at piles of loot should rearrange them, but most
everyone plays with sortloot.  Why not just zap teleport wands?

> [13]: Cursed wands should have some ill effects beside exploding
> occasionally. ("Flames fly from the wand.  The flames scorch your
> boots!  Your boots smoulder!") --Grunt

Seems a lot of effort to come up with bad cases for all wands; what
happens when a wand of wishing misfires?

> [14]: Fireproof candles should not be able to be lit. --Grunt

You mean bug C343-154, "Candles are fireproof."?  It's listed as
"fixed" in the next bug-fix release.

> [15]: Dip things into a potion of gain energy for the same effects as a
> scroll of charging --PsyMar

Overly powerful, imho.  Though, since there's no alchemy way to make
!oGE, maybe not as over-the-top as I first thought.  <shrug>  Makes the
first wish from a wand a little less certain, at least...

> [16]: (15 continued)  The player should get a message like "You dip the
> kitten corpse into the potion of gain energy.  The kitten comes to
> life!  It wields a top hat and cane!  You hear it sing show tunes!  You
> feel like dancing!  You resist!" --aristotle73

Gah.  You had some potential until the top hat and cane; after that,
well, no.

> [19]: Wands of cold can be used to write on water. --aristotle73

Interesting, though I'd imagine that the writing should degrade (melt)
fairly quickly.  And, add in the corrolary YANI: letters frozen in
water have a chance of drifting to a neighboring watery square... >8^}

